  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is forensic science a good career in India? Yes, but only if you understand two things most 12th-graders never hear before enrolling: the real gate into this field is a competitive government recruitment exam, not the degree itself, and the fastest-growing, highest-paying lane right now is digital forensics, not the DNA-lab work most people picture from crime shows.</strong>
    A 2023 law change, the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, now legally requires forensic
    investigation for any crime punishable by seven or more years, and forensic science jobs grew
    roughly 12% a year between 2022 and 2024. Over 700 state forensic lab posts sat unfilled in 2023
    for lack of trained candidates. What most families never budget for: entry-level government lab
    pay is genuinely modest, often Rs 20,000-35,000 a month, hundreds of private colleges now compete
    for a much smaller pool of real exam-qualified openings, and AI is already automating the
    documentation and database work junior forensic staff used to learn on. Building a real
    <strong>skill portfolio</strong>, exam readiness, digital forensics tools, and courtroom
    communication, on top of the degree is what actually unlocks stronger income opportunities and
    moves you toward earlier financial freedom in this field.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Yes, forensic science is a good career in India, but it covers very different tracks, biology/DNA, digital/cyber, toxicology, and forensic accounting, with very different pay and growth speed.</li>
      <li>The real gate is a competitive government recruitment exam, not a licence. A forensic science degree makes you eligible to sit the exam; it does not guarantee a lab seat.</li>
      <li>The BNSS 2023 law mandates forensic investigation for serious crimes, and 700+ state lab vacancies sat unfilled in 2023 for lack of trained candidates, not lack of demand.</li>
      <li>Digital and cyber forensics is the field's clearest growth lane: a roughly 90,000-person talent shortage and close to 40% annual market growth mean specialists there typically earn 30-40% more than traditional lab roles.</li>
      <li><strong>The real decision is not "forensic science or not." It is which specific track fits your actual strengths, and whether you are genuinely preparing for the recruitment exam, or just hoping a degree alone will open the door.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your own fit by completing one real case study, lab or digital, before committing years and an exam-prep budget to this path.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

