  <h2 id="ai-risk">What AI is actually changing for forensic scientists</h2>

  <p>
    Set aside both extremes here too: "forensic work needs human judgment, AI cannot touch it" and
    "AI is coming for every lab job." Neither survives contact with what is actually shifting task by
    task, and this is one of the few fields where credible estimates genuinely disagree on the scale
    of the risk.
  </p>

  <p>
    One automation-risk assessment rates forensic science technicians at roughly 40% automation
    probability, a moderate-risk read. A separate assessment places the same role at around 75%,
    inside a high-risk zone. Both sources agree on which tasks are shrinking fastest: routine
    documentation, database updates, template-based reporting, and chain-of-custody logging. AI tools
    can already accelerate evidence processing meaningfully, speeding up pattern matching, first-pass
    document review, and case-file organisation.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is shrinking</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Manual documentation, database entry, and chain-of-custody logging with no analytical judgment behind it.</li>
        <li>Template-based report drafting and routine, first-pass case-file organisation.</li>
        <li>Repetitive pattern-matching tasks with a single, unambiguous correct answer.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is growing</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Judgment calls about what evidence actually means, and whether a specific method or tool was appropriate for a specific case.</li>
        <li>Courtroom testimony and cross-examination, where a defensible, clearly explained conclusion decides whether evidence survives legal scrutiny.</li>
        <li>Verification of AI-assisted findings before they enter a formal report, catching an automated tool's misread rather than trusting it blindly.</li>
        <li>Digital forensics specialisation, where the talent shortage is currently outpacing automation by a wide margin.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Researchers studying this field describe a similarly staged, practical model: AI speeds up the
    mechanical, repetitive parts of the job, but the reliance on human judgment, interpretation, and
    courtroom testimony still provides real protection against full replacement. The realistic
    takeaway for someone still deciding on this field is a staged path: right now, learn to use
    AI-assisted tools for documentation and first-pass pattern-flagging so routine work takes a
    fraction of the time it used to; as adoption matures over the next few years, the bigger
    opportunity shifts toward owning the verification and testimony layer, catching what an
    automated system misreads, and using the freed-up time for the specialist, judgment-heavy, or
    private-sector digital forensics work that pays more and automates slowest.
  </p>

