  <h2 id="csi-myth">The "CSI effect" myth vs the real job</h2>

  <p>
    A specific fantasy drives a large share of forensic science applications: the idea of walking
    into a crime scene, spotting the one clue everyone else missed, and cracking the case within a
    single dramatic episode.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Researchers and courts actually have a name for the distorted expectations this creates: the
      "CSI effect," where juries and applicants alike expect forensic evidence to work faster and
      more decisively than it genuinely does. The real, achievable version of this career looks much
      less dramatic and far more repeatable: careful sample handling, a backlog of routine cases,
      exact documentation, and eventually one clear specialisation, DNA analysis, toxicology, digital
      forensics, or fraud examination, built over several years inside a government lab or a private
      forensic team. That version is genuinely achievable for a committed candidate. Solving a
      dramatic case within days is not something to plan a career around.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The same myth extends to working conditions worth naming plainly: forensic work regularly
    involves disturbing evidence, autopsy specimens, crime-scene photographs, and case files
    describing violence, and courtroom testimony can involve aggressive, adversarial questioning from
    defence counsel. Neither of those realities makes the field a bad choice, but going in without
    expecting them is a common reason otherwise capable candidates burn out early.
  </p>

