  <h2 id="ceiling">How to actually raise your income ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    A fair salary table only tells you where most people land, not where the strongest outcomes
    actually come from. Forensic science's income ceiling is not fixed by the degree; it is set by
    how far past "entry-level government lab assistant" a person is willing and able to move.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The clearest ceiling-raising moves inside this field are: adding a digital forensics
      certification once core lab or investigative literacy already exists, developing a specialist
      niche like forensic toxicology, questioned documents, or fraud examination, where genuinely few
      candidates are competent, moving from government lab work into a private-sector or consulting
      role once a credible case record exists rather than trying to skip straight to consulting, and
      pairing scientific skill with strong written and verbal communication that makes findings
      genuinely usable in court and boardrooms alike.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of these are guaranteed outcomes, and none happen from the degree alone. They happen for
      candidates who pair technical depth with visible proof, exam-clearing discipline, and enough
      communication skill to defend their own expertise under real scrutiny, not just perform it in a
      classroom.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    On AI specifically, the realistic path is staged, not a single leap. Right now, the useful move
    is learning to work alongside AI-assisted documentation and pattern-flagging tools so routine
    evidence processing takes a fraction of the time it used to. As adoption matures over the next
    few years, the bigger opportunity shifts toward owning the verification and interpretation layer,
    catching what an automated tool misreads, defending a finding under cross-examination, and using
    freed-up time for more specialist or private-sector digital forensics work instead of more
    routine sample processing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="higher-studies">M.Sc, cyber forensics, or CFE: which backup is worth it</h2>

